                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Jeff Kinney</td><td>12</td><td>4.80</td><td>$9.25</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Suzanne Collins</td><td>11</td><td>4.66</td><td>$13.36</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rick Riordan</td><td>11</td><td>4.77</td><td>$9.91</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Gary Chapman</td><td>11</td><td>4.74</td><td>$17.18</td><td>Non-Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>American Psychological Association</td><td>10</td><td>4.50</td><td>$46.00</td><td>Non-Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dr. Seuss</td><td>9</td><td>4.88</td><td>$8.67</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Gallup</td><td>9</td><td>4.00</td><td>$17.00</td><td>Non-Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rob Elliott</td><td>8</td><td>4.56</td><td>$4.00</td><td>Non-Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Stephenie Meyer</td><td>7</td><td>4.66</td><td>$19.86</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dav Pilkey</td><td>7</td><td>4.90</td><td>$6.29</td><td>Fiction</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Pattern Analysis:</strong> Fiction authors dominate the top 10 by appearance count, with 7 of the top 10 authors being Fiction writers. Top-performing authors (Dr. Seuss, Dav Pilkey, Jeff Kinney) combine <strong>low price points ($6–$9)</strong> with <strong>very high ratings (4.80+)</strong>. The only author with a rating below 4.5 (Gallup at 4.00) also carries a relatively high average price ($17).
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title"><span class="section-number">5</span>Strategic Recommendations</h2>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>1. Price Positioning for Bestseller Success</h4>
                <p>The data strongly supports a <strong>$0–$15 price sweet spot</strong> for bestseller performance. Books in this range achieve higher ratings (4.68) and generate more reviews. Pricing above $20 requires exceptional content quality to maintain ratings — the APA manual at $46 and DSM-5 at $105 both show this challenge.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>2. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction Strategy</h4>
                <p>For Fiction titles, <strong>keep prices low</strong> ($8–$11 range) and focus on building review volume — Fiction books with high review counts (15,000+) tend to sustain bestseller status across multiple years. For Non-Fiction, the audience tolerates higher pricing ($14–$17) but demands consistently high quality to maintain ratings above 4.5.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>3. The 2015 Inflection Point — Industry Shift</h4>
                <p>The sharp price decline in 2015 (from $14.60 to $10.42) combined with rising ratings suggests an industry-wide shift toward value-based pricing. This trend has continued through 2019. <strong>Premium pricing strategies face increasing pressure</strong> in the book market.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>4. Author Portfolio Strategy</h4>
                <p>Sustained bestseller success correlates with authors who publish frequently (Jeff Kinney, Rick Riordan, Gary Chapman each have 10+ appearances) at consistent price points. Consider <strong>long-term author relationships</strong> over one-time hits — the data shows consistent performers deliver predictable returns.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>5. Rating Quality Maintenance</h4>
                <p>The negative correlation (r = -0.133) between price and rating indicates that <strong>overpricing creates satisfaction risk</strong>. Books above $20 need to deliver exceptional value perception to justify the price premium. The data shows only the lowest and highest price segments achieve ratings above 4.65.</p>
            </div>
